(()=>{
const H=s=>{let n=2166136261;for(const c of s)n=(n^c.charCodeAt(0))*16777619>>>0;return n>>>0};
const pick=(a,s,i=0)=>a[(H(s)+i*37)%a.length];
const cap=s=>s?s.charAt(0).toUpperCase()+s.slice(1):s;
const branch={
root:{label:'National Crossroads',world:'the constitutional Union before any one future has won the argument',institutions:'the Union cabinet, Parliament, state secretariats, courts, the permanent civil service and institutions that have learned to survive changes of government',human:'chief ministers, MPs, district officers, editors, students, shopkeepers and families who can agree that India should be stronger while disagreeing completely about what strength ought to mean',shadow:'Scale gives India room to absorb mistakes, but it also lets mistakes hide for years inside averages.'},
dem:{label:'Constitutional Republic',world:'a noisy federal democracy where legitimacy is assembled from ballots, bargaining, law and the stubborn independence of state politics',institutions:'coalition leaders, parliamentary committees, state governments, courts, regulators, municipal bodies and a bureaucracy that answers to more than one political clock',human:'voters who change their minds, party workers who know districts better than Delhi does, civil servants, judges, trade unions, associations and regional leaders who expect to be persuaded rather than commanded',shadow:'Consent slows some decisions, yet the right compromise can outlive the government that negotiated it.'},
com:{label:'Revolutionary Republic',world:'a revolutionary India trying to turn anger at hierarchy into a durable socialist order before the revolution becomes another hierarchy of its own',institutions:'workers’ and peasants’ councils, party committees, planning agencies, cooperative federations, public banks and a civil service being stripped down and rebuilt at the same time',human:'mill hands, platform workers, tenant farmers, union organizers, village cooperators, clerks and students who have been promised that politics will finally enter the places where they work and live',shadow:'Mobilization can move mountains of political resistance; concentrated revolutionary authority can also stop inconvenient truths from travelling upward.'},
fas:{label:'National State',world:'an authoritarian nationalist India that treats obedience, spectacle and disciplined unity as substitutes for the untidiness of plural politics',institutions:'central ministries, security agencies, corporatist chambers, information offices, youth organizations and provincial administrations rewarded for conformity',human:'officials learning what may no longer be said, workers whose unions are absorbed into state structures, firms bargaining with political power, loyalists exhilarated by national theatre and minorities forced to calculate the price of being visibly different',shadow:'The regime gains speed by silencing vetoes, then discovers that silenced institutions are also bad at warning leaders when policy is failing.'},
mon:{label:'Imperial Restoration',world:'a modern India deliberately reviving the vocabulary of crown, dynasty and imperial continuity inside a society shaped by republican citizenship',institutions:'a restored court, princely houses, Parliament or an imperial executive, state governments, royal trusts and a civil service asked to reconcile ceremony with modern administration',human:'former royal families, elected politicians, officers, civil servants, regional elites and ordinary citizens deciding whether restoration feels like pageantry, constitutional ballast or the return of inherited privilege',shadow:'Ceremony can bind a political community together, but every expansion of royal authority reopens the question of equality before the state.'},
econ:{label:'Economic Strategy',world:'an India whose economic future is being assembled at once in ports, factory sheds, server halls, bank branches, laboratories, truck stops and household budgets',institutions:'the finance ministry, public banks, state industrial agencies, private firms, regulators, universities, logistics authorities and thousands of small suppliers',human:'machinists, coders, truckers, founders, supervisors, apprentices, shopkeepers, investors and families deciding whether to save, migrate, consume or risk starting something of their own',shadow:'A continental market creates scale, but scale becomes productivity only when power, logistics, finance, skills and trust arrive in the same place at the same time.'},
soc:{label:'Society & Human Capital',world:'the India encountered before grand strategy—in classrooms, clinics, buses, rented rooms, examination halls, police counters and the search for a first stable job',institutions:'state departments, municipal corporations, school systems, hospitals, universities, welfare agencies and local governments whose quality varies enormously from district to district',human:'teachers, nurses, parents, commuters, young graduates, women negotiating entry into paid work, renters and children for whom the state is experienced as a queue, a form, a bus route or a person behind a desk',shadow:'India can produce institutions of astonishing excellence while leaving the median citizen dependent on systems that are merely adequate or unpredictably absent.'},
clim:{label:'Climate & Material Resilience',world:'a monsoon civilization entering a century in which heat, water, crops and electricity increasingly decide what every other policy is allowed to accomplish',institutions:'power utilities, irrigation departments, agricultural universities, weather agencies, city governments, disaster authorities and finance ministries',human:'farmers watching cloud cover, line workers repairing transformers in heat, apartment residents waiting for tankers, municipal engineers reading drainage maps and coastal families who compare each season with the one before it',shadow:'Growth creates the money needed for adaptation even as it creates more demand for land, cooling, power and water.'},
dip:{label:'Diplomacy & Maritime State',world:'an India determined to keep freedom of action in a world where sea lanes, technology rules, finance, migration and neighbouring politics bind countries together more tightly every year',institutions:'the foreign ministry, embassies, ports, development agencies, state governments, coast-guard institutions and economic ministries that increasingly practice foreign policy together',human:'diplomats, sailors, traders, students, overseas Indians, port workers and border communities who experience international politics as visas, prices, freight, security and opportunity',shadow:'Strategic autonomy is not distance from everyone else; it is the expensive ability to maintain enough relationships and capabilities that refusal remains a real option.'},
space:{label:'Space Strategy',world:'a space programme leaving the age of singular national firsts and entering the harder age of cadence, safety, suppliers and institutional memory',institutions:'ISRO centres, launch ranges, universities, private space firms, component suppliers, mission-control rooms and research laboratories',human:'engineers on night shifts, technicians signing test sheets, scientists protecting instrument time, students choosing aerospace careers and families for whom a launch is becoming familiar without becoming ordinary',shadow:'A spectacular mission can create prestige in a day; space power exists only when the next mission is easier to organize, safer to fly and less dependent on heroic improvisation.'}
};
const domain=[];const D=(re,data)=>domain.push({re,...data});
D(/crossroads|constitutional-question/i,{name:'the national choice',scene:['Before the cabinet folders acquire coloured tabs and before television studios decide which future is inevitable, India is still all of its possibilities at once. The morning trains run, state assemblies quarrel, courts open, markets clear and launch teams work through checklists while the political class argues about what sort of country all of this activity is supposed to create.','The decisive room is not one room. It is a chain of rooms—from South Block to state secretariats, party offices, district collectorates and family dining tables—where the same question is asked in different vocabularies: what must India preserve, and what is it willing to break in order to become more powerful?'],material:'constitutional rules, coalition arithmetic, state power, administrative continuity and the accumulated expectations of citizens who have lived under the Republic long enough to treat some things as permanent',people:'politicians who want a mandate, officials who want continuity, activists who want rupture, business leaders who want predictability and voters who may want several contradictory things at once',argument:'The choice is not between movement and stasis. Every path changes India; the real dispute is over who may authorize change, who bears its cost and which institutions are allowed to say no.',risk:'Once a political order is chosen, later reforms inherit its habits. A democracy learns to bargain, a revolutionary state learns to mobilize, an authoritarian state learns to command and an empire learns to stage authority.',outside:'Foreign governments do not need to know the exact focus tree to understand the stakes. They watch the durability of institutions, the treatment of dissent, the direction of economic policy and whether regional promises still mean what they meant yesterday.',memory:'Years later, historians will be tempted to describe the winning path as obvious. It will not have felt obvious to the people making the decision.'});
D(/coalition/i,{name:'coalition government',scene:['Long before a bill reaches the floor of Parliament, the argument has already crossed phone calls, state guest houses, party offices and late-night meetings where a single regional demand can hold up a national timetable. Coalition government is not arithmetic after all; it is the management of political memory.','A cabinet meeting looks national on television, but every allied minister arrives carrying a state, a party organization and a promise made somewhere far from Delhi. The government learns that a coalition is strongest when those promises are negotiated before they become public ultimatums.'],material:'cabinet committees, common-minimum agreements, state-specific projects, committee chairs, budget bargaining and the calendars of allies who must face their own voters',people:'regional leaders who refuse to become decorative partners, ministers trying to keep national programs coherent, civil servants rewriting proposals for the third time and voters who expect coalition participation to produce something tangible',argument:'The wager is that bargaining can be institutionalized without turning every vote into an auction. A durable coalition shares authorship of policy instead of merely sharing office.',risk:'Compromise can make policy durable, but it can also produce programs so carefully balanced that nobody is responsible for their failures.',outside:'Investors and foreign partners may miss the simplicity of a single dominant mandate, yet they also discover that agreements endorsed across several parties can survive political turnover more easily.',memory:'If it works, coalition politics stops being remembered as a period of weakness and begins to look like the operating system of a genuinely federal power.'});
D(/federal|states|accession/i,{name:'federal power',scene:['India is too large for New Delhi to be the only place where national strategy is made. In Chennai, Mumbai, Lucknow, Guwahati and dozens of other capitals, chief ministers govern populations comparable to sovereign states and possess their own bureaucracies, political mandates and memories of central interference.','A Union circular can travel across the country in seconds; implementation cannot. By the time a national program reaches a district office, it has passed through state budgets, local politics, language, staffing and the practical judgement of officials who know which parts of the order can actually be carried out.'],material:'tax devolution, grants, state borrowing, regulatory waivers, centrally sponsored schemes, inter-state councils and the division of responsibility that determines who gets blamed when delivery fails',people:'chief ministers guarding autonomy, Union ministers seeking coherence, state civil servants translating national schemes into local realities and citizens who rarely care which level of government was technically responsible',argument:'Federalism here is not politeness toward the states. It is a theory of capacity: national ambition becomes more credible when states have enough authority and incentive to adapt it rather than merely comply with it.',risk:'The Union can gain legitimacy by sharing power, but it also loses the comfort of uniformity. Successful states may pull ahead; weak ones may demand rescue; political rivals may use autonomy to obstruct national priorities.',outside:'Neighbours and investors often discover that dealing with India means dealing with several governments at once. That complexity can frustrate negotiation, but it can also create multiple durable points of contact.',memory:'A stronger federation will not look tidier on a map. Its success will be measured in whether diversity produces experimentation rather than paralysis.'});
D(/civic|multilingual|language|cultural-republic|culture/i,{name:'belonging and culture',scene:['India speaks before it legislates. Railway announcements change language from state to state; films cross linguistic borders with subtitles and songs; courtrooms, classrooms and government counters negotiate daily between the language of administration and the language in which a citizen actually thinks.','National identity is never encountered in the abstract. It is sung, printed, translated, filmed, prayed, joked about and argued over. A policy on language or culture therefore reaches places where a fiscal rule never will: family memory, schoolbooks, street signs and the private sense of whether the state sounds like home.'],material:'translation services, school curricula, public broadcasting, archives, arts funding, language technology, signage and the thousands of small administrative choices that determine whose words appear first',people:'teachers, translators, artists, parents, students, civil servants and communities that can be intensely patriotic while resisting the idea that patriotism requires cultural sameness',argument:'The policy asks whether national cohesion is best built by reducing difference or by making the state competent enough to live with difference confidently.',risk:'A celebration of pluralism can become empty festival language if institutions remain monolingual or culturally distant. A drive for uniformity can create the very alienation it claims to cure.',outside:'India’s cultural influence abroad grows partly from the fact that there is no single Indian culture to export. The same diversity that complicates administration multiplies the country’s channels of connection.',memory:'What endures will be less the slogan than the ordinary feeling of whether citizens could meet the Union without first translating themselves into somebody else’s India.'});
D(/rights|courts|legal/i,{name:'rights and justice',scene:['The Constitution is majestic in print and much less majestic at a crowded lower court on a humid weekday. Files move from desk to desk; litigants wait in corridors; a lawyer explains another adjournment; somewhere else an overworked legal-aid office decides which emergency must come first.','Rights become believable in the unphotogenic places where the state is asked to restrain itself. A police station, a magistrate’s court, a tribunal counter and an appeals registry matter more to constitutional culture than a thousand ceremonial speeches about the rule of law.'],material:'judge vacancies, case management, legal aid, forensic capacity, translation, court infrastructure, police procedure and the incentives that reward delay as often as they punish it',people:'litigants who cannot afford years of uncertainty, judges buried under caseloads, lawyers whose livelihoods may depend on procedure, police officers under pressure for quick results and citizens who discover the Constitution only when something has gone wrong',argument:'The reform is about making rights usable rather than merely admirable. Speed matters, but speed without fairness can turn administrative efficiency into a faster way of making mistakes.',risk:'Judicial reform can be sold as a technical problem until it touches power. The moment courts become faster and more accessible, old interests discover that delay had been serving them.',outside:'Commercial partners care about contract enforcement; human-rights observers care about due process; ordinary citizens care about whether the same institutions work when nobody important is watching.',memory:'A constitutional republic is remembered less for the rights it wrote down than for the number of people who could actually invoke them.'});
D(/developmental-democracy|development-ledger|productive-century/i,{name:'development and measurement',scene:['A ministry can count kilometres of road, rupees allocated and schemes launched. It is harder to count the hour a commuter gets back each day, the shipment that no longer spoils, the class that finally has a teacher or the small firm that can now trust the electricity supply.','The new files look different: fewer photographs of inaugurations, more columns asking what changed after the ribbon was cut. Departments that are used to measuring activity are forced to defend outcomes.'],material:'travel times, freight costs, learning levels, power reliability, health outcomes, household income, project maintenance and the uncomfortable comparison between money spent and capability created',people:'ministers who want visible achievements, auditors who ask what endured, local officials who know why targets were missed and citizens who can tell immediately when a metric flatters a service they use every day',argument:'Development becomes a discipline of evidence rather than a parade of inputs. The political wager is that voters can be offered proof of competence instead of only proof of expenditure.',risk:'Metrics can improve government or merely improve the art of gaming metrics. Once a number becomes a target, whole bureaucracies learn to make the number look good.',outside:'Development banks, investors and other governments watch the same shift because reliable measurement makes India easier to trust—and easier to compare.',memory:'If the ledger survives changes of government, it may become one of those dull institutions future generations assume was always there.'});
D(/bangladesh|bengal/i,{name:'the Bengal world',scene:['East of the narrow Siliguri corridor, the map stops behaving like a set of clean national colours. The Ganges becomes the Padma, Bengali is spoken on both sides of a sovereign frontier, freight routes remember older geographies and every monsoon reminds planners that rivers are indifferent to customs posts.','At dawn near the border, trucks queue beside tea stalls while passenger trains and family histories point toward a Bengal larger than either state can comfortably claim. Dhaka and Kolkata are separate political centres inside one dense ecological and cultural region.'],material:'river-sharing data, customs yards, rail links, textile supply chains, power trading, port access, border markets and the political sensitivities of West Bengal and India’s northeast',people:'Bangladeshi officials alert to hierarchy, Indian border communities tired of being treated as abstractions, traders who want predictable crossings, families with memories on both sides and politicians who know that Bengali identity cannot simply be nationalized away',argument:'Any Indian project in Bengal has to answer one question before all others: is Bangladesh being treated as a partner with agency, or as geography to be arranged around Indian convenience?',risk:'Cooperation can deepen so quickly that sovereignty becomes politically sensitive; coercion can produce the opposite result, turning every practical dispute into proof that the larger neighbour cannot be trusted.',outside:'Dhaka will look to other partners whenever Indian influence feels suffocating. Beijing, Gulf states, Japan and Western governments all become more relevant when New Delhi forgets that proximity does not equal consent.',memory:'The success or failure of the relationship will be remembered in bridges, train timetables, river agreements and the tone in which people on each side speak about the other.'});
D(/nepal|himalay|mountain/i,{name:'the Himalayan relationship',scene:['The road north rises slowly and then all at once. Plains give way to river valleys, landslides close routes without asking permission, and the political map of the Himalaya is overlaid by pilgrimage, kinship, hydropower, migration and an open border that millions experience more casually than diplomats do.','Kathmandu is close to India by geography and fiercely attentive to every reminder of that closeness. A proposal that looks generous in New Delhi can sound paternal in Nepal within hours, especially when older memories of pressure are revived in parliament or the press.'],material:'hydropower contracts, transmission lines, mountain roads, open-border movement, labour protections, pilgrimage routes, disaster warnings and the slow logistics of high-altitude infrastructure',people:'Nepali workers crossing for jobs, Indian and Nepali traders, families spread across the border, hydropower planners, local politicians and national leaders who cannot afford to appear subordinate',argument:'The question is whether interdependence can be made dignified. India already has influence; the difficult task is using it without constantly reminding Nepal of the imbalance.',risk:'Heavy-handed policy can turn even useful projects into symbols of dependency. Excessive caution can create a vacuum that others are happy to fill.',outside:'China is always present in the Himalayan strategic imagination, even where it is not physically present in a project. Kathmandu uses that fact to widen its room for manoeuvre.',memory:'The relationship will be judged by whether Nepalis remember Indian proximity as opportunity, pressure, or—most likely—a complicated mixture of both.'});
D(/mal[eé]|maldives|island-solidarity|island|oceanic-monarchy|ocean-community|indian-ocean-state|workers-ocean/i,{name:'the Indian Ocean islands',scene:['Southwest of the subcontinent, the land breaks apart into water. On a Maldivian atoll the ocean is not the blank blue between strategic arrows; it is the road to the next island, the source of food and tourism, the route of undersea cables and the climate threat visible at the edge of the street.','A giant country looks different from a small island state. Indian aircraft, credit, medical access or coast-guard support can be indispensable one month and politically radioactive the next if Malé believes assistance is becoming entitlement.'],material:'harbours, desalination, tourism receipts, air links, undersea cables, coast-guard coordination, climate finance and islands only metres above present sea level',people:'island councillors, resort workers, fishermen, students, climate planners, Maldivian politicians balancing nationalism against practical dependence and Indian officials learning that generosity does not purchase gratitude',argument:'Maritime influence succeeds when smaller states can use Indian power without feeling swallowed by it. Protectorate or annexation paths deliberately break that bargain and inherit the resistance that follows.',risk:'Coercion may seize a strategic position while destroying the political legitimacy that made the position useful. Even peaceful association becomes unstable if local consent is treated as a formality.',outside:'Other powers do not need to match India ship for ship to complicate its position; they need only offer Malé an alternative whenever New Delhi becomes overbearing.',memory:'Island politics will remember not the size of India’s promises but whether people felt safer, freer and more prosperous—or merely more supervised.'});
D(/pakistan|western-reckoning|subdue-pakistan/i,{name:'the western frontier',scene:['To the west, policy never arrives alone. It arrives with Partition, wars, the line through Punjab, closed trade routes, Kashmir, divided families and decades of security doctrine already sitting in the room before the first official speaks.','A phrase written for domestic applause in Delhi can be replayed in Islamabad as evidence of intent before the speech is over. Both states possess institutions trained to expect the worst from the other, which means symbolic moves can acquire material consequences with frightening speed.'],material:'border crossings, airspace, river systems, intelligence assessments, trade bans, military signalling, diplomatic channels and the civilian relationships repeatedly frozen by crises',people:'Punjabi families with memories on both sides, traders who have lost markets, soldiers and police on the frontier, diplomats trying to keep channels open and publics that have been taught several incompatible versions of the same history',argument:'Any path that treats Pakistan as an unfinished territorial problem turns historical grievance into a permanent governing commitment. Victory on a map would not make millions of people politically disappear.',risk:'Escalation can move faster than political leaders expect, and coercive control would impose security, administrative and legitimacy costs far beyond the moment of conquest.',outside:'China, Gulf partners, the United States and other powers would all recalculate. A subcontinental conflict does not remain subcontinental once nuclear risk, trade and great-power alignment enter the equation.',memory:'The western relationship has enough history already. The darkest routes add another generation that must live inside choices made for strategic drama.'});
D(/china|dragon|asian-hegemony|asian-balance/i,{name:'the China rivalry',scene:['China is not one problem for India. It is a mountain road in Ladakh, a container of electronics in Mumbai, a telecom standard, a factory comparison, a naval map, a multilateral vote and a quiet argument over which Asian giant better represents the future.','On the Himalayan frontier, distance is measured in hours of difficult logistics. In the economic ministries, it is measured in import dependence and production capacity. In diplomacy, it is measured in how many partners are willing to work with India without asking it to become somebody else’s ally.'],material:'border infrastructure, manufacturing ecosystems, critical imports, technology standards, ports, research capacity, diplomatic coalitions and the enormous fiscal cost of trying to compete everywhere at once',people:'mountain communities, soldiers, engineers, importers, manufacturers, diplomats, students and political leaders who can admire Chinese capacity while rejecting the idea that India must imitate Chinese political institutions',argument:'The most serious challenge to China is not a slogan. It is the creation of enough economic, technological and institutional weight that India can disagree without being strategically cornered.',risk:'Competition can sharpen capability; obsession can distort priorities until every domestic policy is judged only by whether Beijing would approve or fear it.',outside:'Beijing reads Indian moves as part of a wider system involving the United States, Russia, the Indian Ocean, South Asia and global technology networks. It will answer pressure with pressure and partnership with selective partnership.',memory:'The rivalry will define an era only if both countries allow it to. India’s deeper test is whether competition with China enlarges its own capabilities or shrinks its imagination.'});
D(/sri-lanka|ceylon/i,{name:'the Palk Strait and Sri Lanka',scene:['Across the Palk Strait, the distance between India and Sri Lanka is small enough for fishing boats, weather systems, pilgrimage and Tamil politics to ignore the clean lines of diplomatic protocol. Colombo is foreign policy; Tamil Nadu makes sure it is also domestic politics.','Ports loom large on strategic maps, but the relationship is lived in ferries, tourism, fisheries disputes, debt negotiations and the memories of a civil war whose political echoes never fully disappeared from southern India.'],material:'ports, ferry links, fisheries, shipping lanes, tourism, credit, energy projects and the constant need to coordinate Delhi’s strategic priorities with politics in Tamil Nadu',people:'Sri Lankan officials guarding room for manoeuvre, Tamil fishing communities, port workers, business owners, diplomats and Indian politicians who know that coercion across the strait will be debated at home as well as abroad',argument:'India can be Sri Lanka’s most natural partner without being its only partner. The difference between influence and domination is therefore the central strategic test.',risk:'Overt pressure encourages precisely the balancing behaviour it is meant to prevent, giving outside powers new political space in Colombo.',outside:'China, Japan, Gulf investors and Western governments all matter because Sri Lanka has choices. Indian geography creates advantage, not a monopoly.',memory:'The strait will remain after governments change. A durable policy is one that leaves the next generation more connected without feeling less sovereign.'});
D(/peoples-revolution|workers-peasants|council-state|red-federalism/i,{name:'revolutionary power',scene:['The revolution is loud in the squares and much quieter the morning after, when ministries still need payrolls, trains still need dispatchers and a village council has to decide whether the old district officer is now an enemy, an employee or simply the only person who knows where the records are kept.','Factory gates and university campuses become political classrooms. Meetings run late. New committees claim authority from mass participation while older institutions continue to possess the files, expertise and routines that keep a continental state functioning.'],material:'union halls, local councils, party committees, food distribution, payroll systems, district records, public banks and the hurried rewriting of administrative authority',people:'workers thrilled by a new sense of power, peasants demanding that revolutionary language reach the countryside, old officials negotiating their survival, students pressing for faster change and families wondering whether the new order will widen freedom or simply change who gives orders',argument:'The revolution promises that ordinary people will enter the state rather than merely petition it. Its credibility depends on whether councils acquire real power without creating a maze in which nobody can be held responsible.',risk:'The language of emergency can justify temporary concentration of power so easily that temporary structures become permanent before anyone notices.',outside:'Foreign governments and capital markets react to the rupture, but the decisive question is domestic: can the new order keep food moving, wages paid and disagreement possible while reconstructing authority?',memory:'Revolutions are remembered through symbols; they survive through routines. The gap between those two things is where this path will succeed or fail.'});
D(/socialise|planned-industrialisation|land-tiller|cooperative-republic|revolutionary-culture|delhi-international/i,{name:'socialist reconstruction',scene:['The revolutionary slogans eventually reach the ledger books. Ownership records are opened, boardrooms are summoned, land registers are disputed and planning offices begin the immense task of deciding which needs should become national priorities and which prices are still allowed to speak.','At a cooperative meeting, the language is more concrete than ideology: who owns the machine, who gets credit, who sets the procurement price, who carries the loss after a bad season, and whether a public manager can be removed when everybody knows the manager was appointed by the party.'],material:'land records, public banks, procurement systems, production targets, cooperative bylaws, price controls, industrial inventories and data systems expected to know more about the economy than they actually do',people:'farmers with unequal plots, factory workers expecting control rather than just new bosses, managers who possess scarce technical knowledge, cooperative organizers and consumers who discover that equality does not automatically stock a shop',argument:'Social ownership is treated as a means rather than a magic word. The real struggle is over how power inside economic institutions is distributed after private ownership is reduced.',risk:'Planning can mobilize capital for neglected goals, but bad information and political fear can make errors persist long after a market signal would have exposed them.',outside:'Trade partners and investors adjust quickly, while other socialist governments watch to see whether India is inventing a genuinely different model or reproducing older command systems at larger scale.',memory:'The legacy will be judged not by the percentage of assets nationalized but by whether workers and farmers actually felt less disposable inside the new economy.'});
D(/national-state|one-state-one-will|corporate-state|national-labour|youth-of-nation|information-directorate|cultural-unification|monumental|demographic-state|demographic-policy|martial-republic/i,{name:'authoritarian consolidation',scene:['The new order does not arrive everywhere as a police raid. It also arrives as a revised school ceremony, a compulsory organization at work, a slogan on a ministry wall, a television producer receiving a phone call and an official deciding that a troublesome statistic can wait until after the speech.','The capital grows theatrical. Uniform visual language spreads across ministries and rallies. The state promises an end to drift and bargaining, and many supporters experience the first months as exhilarating proof that somebody is finally in command.'],material:'executive decrees, censorship rules, corporatist bargaining, youth organizations, public ceremonies, personnel vetting, security files and administrative targets that become harder to question as they become more political',people:'loyalists who feel history accelerating, workers whose independent organizations are absorbed, managers who learn the value of political access, teachers asked to carry ideology into classrooms and minorities who discover that “unity” is being defined around somebody else’s norm',argument:'The regime claims that plural politics wastes national energy. Its answer is to collapse disagreement into administration: once the state has chosen, opposition becomes not an alternative but an obstacle.',risk:'Command produces visible speed while degrading the information system on which good command depends. Officials who fear punishment for bad news eventually learn to manufacture good news.',outside:'Foreign partners may admire decisiveness in narrow transactions while growing wary of repression, unpredictability and a nationalism that increasingly interprets compromise as weakness.',memory:'Authoritarian eras build monuments precisely because they fear forgetting. Whether those monuments later symbolize order, coercion or both will depend on the lives lived beneath them.'});
D(/subcontinent-doctrine|ultimatum|annex|subdue|dominate|control-sri|eastern-settlement|himalayan-command/i,{name:'coercive expansion',scene:['On the wall map, the arrow is clean. On the ground, an ultimatum arrives in another country’s parliament, on another country’s radio stations and in the homes of people who did not participate in the strategic theory that now claims authority over them.','The regime’s language shifts from influence to obedience. Diplomatic notes become deadlines; military readiness becomes political theatre; administrators quietly begin asking what happens the day after a neighbour is forced to accept the new order.'],material:'sanctions risk, occupation budgets, border administration, intelligence, diplomatic isolation, disrupted trade, resistance and the enormous gap between controlling a capital and governing a society',people:'soldiers and officials asked to carry out policy, nationalist supporters expecting quick triumph, civilians in the targeted state defending sovereignty and Indian families discovering that imperial ambition has recurring fiscal and human costs',argument:'Territory and submission are treated as proofs of strength. The darker truth is that every coerced population becomes a new political problem the state has chosen to own.',risk:'Initial military or diplomatic success can hide the long afterlife of resistance, sanctions, insurgency, policing and international balancing.',outside:'Neighbours who once disagreed with one another acquire a common reason to cooperate. Great powers gain new openings. Even friendly governments begin planning for an India that may no longer regard restraint as a virtue.',memory:'Conquest writes dramatic dates into textbooks. Occupation writes years of smaller dates into family histories.'});
D(/princes-return|crown-settlement|constitutional-crown|executive-empire|chamber-princes|imperial-senate|royal-civil-service|imperial-capital/i,{name:'the restored crown',scene:['Old palaces were never entirely empty; many survived as hotels, museums, family trusts and memories of a political order that independence had deliberately placed behind the Republic. Restoration makes those memories constitutional again.','Ceremony begins harmlessly enough: invitations, titles, uniforms, processions, a revived court calendar. Then a question appears on a government file—what, exactly, may the Crown do? At that moment nostalgia becomes law.'],material:'succession rules, royal finances, ceremonial precedence, parliamentary authority, state representation, civil-service oaths and the legal boundary between a crown that symbolizes India and a crown that can command it',people:'former princely families rediscovering public relevance, republicans alarmed by hereditary privilege, citizens attracted to spectacle, civil servants adapting old-new protocol and regional elites negotiating how much status restoration returns to them',argument:'The restorationists claim that a crown can embody historical depth beyond party conflict. Their opponents reply that republican equality was not a decorative choice to be revised for atmosphere.',risk:'A constitutional monarch may become a stabilizing symbol; an executive monarch turns every policy dispute into a dispute over inherited authority.',outside:'Other states treat the experiment with fascination, but investors and diplomats care about the practical question beneath the robes: who actually has the final signature?',memory:'If the crown survives, later generations may find it ordinary. That normality would be the restoration’s greatest victory—and the Republic’s most profound transformation.'});
D(/oceanic-monarchy|protectorate|ceylon-station|bengal-question|eastern-marches|imperial-railways|crown-economy|asian-concert|empire-of-india/i,{name:'imperial statecraft',scene:['Imperial politics is made of routes as much as titles. Railways, sea lanes, treaty ports and ceremonial visits are used to make distant places feel connected to the restored centre, even when the people living there understand the relationship very differently.','A royal train crossing the plains is easy to photograph. The harder work happens in agreements over customs, representation, budgets and sovereignty—questions that reveal whether “imperial community” is a federation people enter or a hierarchy they are expected to accept.'],material:'rail corridors, royal development trusts, treaty rights, port privileges, federal representation, protectorate agreements, public finance and the ceremonial machinery used to make unequal arrangements look natural',people:'royal advisers, elected politicians, railway workers, port officials, provincial elites and neighbouring citizens who may enjoy easier movement while rejecting the idea that convenience creates political allegiance',argument:'The empire tries to convert historical symbolism into twenty-first-century integration. Its legitimacy depends on whether membership is negotiated, inherited or imposed.',risk:'Prestige infrastructure can knit a system together, but it can also become an expensive stage set concealing unresolved questions of representation and consent.',outside:'Asian governments distinguish carefully between an India offering concert and an India demanding deference. The restored crown does not erase modern nationalism beyond India’s borders.',memory:'Empires are often remembered through maps. The people who lived inside them remember paperwork, taxes, journeys, opportunities and humiliations.'});
D(/factory-world|supplier-nation|export-discipline|corridor-republic|ports-first|semiconductor/i,{name:'manufacturing power',scene:['Before sunrise at an industrial estate, the grand theory of manufacturing is already awake. Buses unload workers, trucks wait for gates to open, a supplier checks a batch against a tolerance sheet and a purchasing manager decides whether the late shipment will stop an entire line.','The flagship factory gets the photograph. The harder economy lives behind it: die-makers, tool shops, chemical suppliers, logistics firms, quality labs and technicians whose names never appear in investment announcements.'],material:'machine tools, supplier contracts, customs clearance, port dwell time, working capital, power quality, industrial training and the unforgiving arithmetic of defects and delivery dates',people:'shift workers, production engineers, small suppliers risking family capital on one new machine, logistics managers, state industrial officials and exporters who learn that foreign customers do not care how difficult the domestic environment was',argument:'India is not choosing whether to have factories; it is choosing whether manufacturing becomes an ecosystem dense enough that each new factory makes the next one easier to build.',risk:'Subsidies can attract visible plants without creating supplier depth. Protection can nurture firms or teach them to survive without becoming internationally competitive.',outside:'Customers in Europe, East Asia, the Gulf and North America measure Indian industrial ambition in quality, cost and reliability rather than speeches about scale.',memory:'Industrial revolutions are eventually remembered as statistics. While they are happening, they are remembered as shift schedules, migration, noise, new wages and the first family member who learns to operate a machine nobody in the district had seen ten years earlier.'});
D(/digital|compute|services|research-cities/i,{name:'the digital economy',scene:['The new industrial landscape can be almost silent. In Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune, Gurugram and emerging research corridors, value moves through fibre, code repositories, design files and server halls whose electricity demand is more visible than the work happening inside them.','A young engineer can sell an hour of expertise across an ocean without a container leaving port. That advantage is immense, but it can also tempt the country to confuse a world-class professional class with broad technological depth.'],material:'data centres, electricity, chips, cloud contracts, universities, research grants, digital standards, venture finance and the labour market that decides whether talented people build companies in India or leave',people:'software engineers, designers, call-centre workers, founders, researchers, public-sector technologists and graduates who discover that the difference between opportunity and underemployment can be one credential, one city or one reliable internet connection',argument:'Digital primacy asks India to turn talent into institutions, products and intellectual property rather than remain merely the world’s extraordinarily capable back office.',risk:'Services can grow without employing enough people; data infrastructure can concentrate power; an AI boom can widen the distance between elite technical labour and the rest of the workforce.',outside:'Global customers prize Indian talent but can move contracts quickly. Durable power comes when Indian firms own platforms, standards and products rather than only providing labour to somebody else’s system.',memory:'The digital transformation will be remembered not by the number of apps launched but by whether technical capability spread beyond a handful of metropolitan islands.'});
D(/domestic-market|million-firms|mass-consumption|regional-industrialism|cooperative-credit/i,{name:'the domestic market',scene:['India’s largest economic asset does not sit in a ministry vault. It is dispersed across hundreds of millions of households deciding whether this is the year to buy a scooter, move to a city, replace a phone, educate a daughter longer, renovate a shop or hire one more worker.','In a small industrial town, growth does not look like an export terminal. It looks like a workshop moving into a second shed, a cooperative bank approving equipment finance and a retailer ordering inventory because local wages have become just a little more predictable.'],material:'household income, small-business credit, local roads, electricity, retail demand, GST compliance, regional clusters and the delicate cash flow of firms too small to survive long payment delays',people:'shop owners, workshop proprietors, self-employed families, migrant workers, cooperative bankers and consumers whose cautious spending collectively determines whether domestic scale becomes an engine or merely a statistic',argument:'The strategy bets that broad demand can pull productivity outward from the metropolitan core, giving millions of firms a reason to formalize, invest and specialize.',risk:'Consumption without productivity can become inflation and imports. Easy credit can preserve weak firms as easily as it finances strong ones.',outside:'Foreign companies are drawn by India’s market, but the national objective is to ensure that market size also builds Indian capability rather than only attracting finished goods.',memory:'A domestic-market transformation is rarely remembered through one mega-project. It is remembered when a town that used to lose its young people begins to have reasons for them to stay.'});
D(/state-heavy|steel-and-machines|public-finance|strategic-sectors|energy-industrial-core/i,{name:'state-directed industry',scene:['Heavy industry begins with weight: steel coils, turbines, forgings, machine beds, transformers and the enormous pieces of equipment that determine what an economy can physically make for itself.','In planning rooms, the problem is measured in decades. Private finance prefers projects that reveal returns sooner; the state asks whether waiting for the market will leave India permanently dependent on other countries for the tools of industrial independence.'],material:'development-bank balance sheets, steel capacity, capital-goods plants, grid investment, procurement guarantees, technology licensing and the patient engineering work required to move from assembling equipment to designing it',people:'plant engineers, public-bank officers, construction workers, procurement officials, private manufacturers worried about state competition and taxpayers who ultimately carry the risk when strategic bets fail',argument:'The directed state accepts lower short-term financial elegance in exchange for capabilities it considers too important to leave entirely to private return calculations.',risk:'Patient capital can create industries markets neglect; political capital can also protect expensive failures long after technical evidence says a project should change course.',outside:'Technology suppliers and strategic partners see both a customer and a future competitor. Import dependence falls only if domestic firms master design, maintenance and tooling rather than merely receive public orders.',memory:'The monuments of heavy industry are not subtle. Their deeper legacy is whether the rest of the economy eventually buys better machines because those plants existed.'});
D(/human-capital|ordinary-school|district-health|women-work|urban-century|housing-compact/i,{name:'ordinary state capacity',scene:['The national promise arrives at human scale: a classroom fan turning through an afternoon lesson, a nurse checking an inventory sheet, a woman calculating whether the last bus home is safe, a renter comparing wages with a deposit, a graduate refreshing a job portal.','None of these scenes belongs on a strategic map, yet together they decide how much of India’s population becomes usable capability rather than frustrated potential.'],material:'teacher attendance, clinic staffing, bus frequency, childcare, street lighting, rental supply, vocational pathways and municipal budgets whose small failures compound into lost years of human time',people:'students, parents, nurses, teachers, commuters, women entering paid work, migrants and municipal employees who rarely describe their problems as “human capital” even when economists do',argument:'The state is being asked to make the ordinary reliable. Excellence at the top no longer excuses fragility in the median school, clinic or neighbourhood.',risk:'Large national schemes can create impressive coverage while leaving quality untouched. Local reform can work beautifully in one state and disappear at the border of the next.',outside:'India’s demographic advantage is discussed constantly abroad, but demography is only potential until health, education, mobility and employment convert it into skill.',memory:'The most successful social focus will be almost invisible in retrospect: a generation simply expects services that its parents once treated as uncertain.'});
D(/monsoon|water-security|climate-agriculture|energy-resilience/i,{name:'climate and resilience',scene:['The sky still organizes Indian life. A delayed monsoon changes reservoir calculations, sowing decisions, electricity demand and political anxiety before it becomes a headline. During a heat wave, a transformer failure is not an engineering statistic; it is a factory stopping, a pump failing and a family trying to sleep in a room that never cools.','Climate change arrives as pressure on systems that already have histories: irrigation canals designed for older rainfall patterns, city drains buried beneath new construction, crop calendars inherited from parents and grids asked to serve far more cooling than their planners imagined.'],material:'reservoir levels, aquifers, crop varieties, weather forecasting, grid reserves, transformers, cooling demand, insurance, drainage and the financing of infrastructure whose success is measured by disasters that do not happen',people:'farmers reading the sky, agricultural scientists, municipal engineers, power-line crews, apartment residents, factory managers and low-income workers who bear the greatest cost when heat or water turns unreliable',argument:'Resilience means spending political money before a crisis, when avoided damage is difficult to photograph and maintenance has no ribbon-cutting ceremony.',risk:'Adaptation can protect growth while also becoming a competition over whose neighbourhood, crop or industry receives scarce water and power first.',outside:'Energy markets, climate finance and upstream river politics ensure that resilience is partly international even when the suffering from failure is intensely local.',memory:'Future citizens may never know which flood did not happen because a drain was widened or which harvest survived because a seed system changed. That anonymity is the quiet success this strategy is buying.'});
D(/strategic-autonomy|neighbourhood-first|global-south/i,{name:'strategic autonomy',scene:['Foreign policy is easiest to romanticize from a podium. In practice it is a calendar crowded with incompatible partners: a technology agreement here, discounted energy there, a defence exercise elsewhere, a climate coalition with countries that disagree on almost everything else.','Indian diplomacy works best when outsiders stop asking which camp India belongs to and start asking what India can contribute, withhold or convene. That requires assets behind the posture.'],material:'trade access, energy supply, defence production, technology partnerships, development finance, diplomatic staffing and the credibility to sustain several important relationships without allowing any one of them to become indispensable',people:'diplomats juggling contradictory files, businesses exposed to sanctions or standards, students moving across visa regimes, diaspora communities and border populations whose lives can change because of a distant bilateral dispute',argument:'Autonomy is treated as capability rather than equidistance. The country must build enough options that choosing one partner on one issue does not become permanent alignment on all issues.',risk:'Trying to keep every door open can look like indecision; declaring too much independence without the material means to sustain it can turn autonomy into rhetoric.',outside:'Major powers test India’s flexibility constantly. Smaller states judge whether New Delhi’s language of multipolarity leaves genuine space for them too.',memory:'The doctrine succeeds when later governments inherit room to choose, not merely a collection of relationships they are afraid to disturb.'});
D(/space-question|humans-first|astronaut|gaganyaan|orbital-station|crewed-lunar/i,{name:'human spaceflight',scene:['At Sriharikota, the public countdown begins late. The real countdown has been running for years in weld inspections, parachute tests, medical protocols, software reviews and meetings where an engineer has to be able to say “no” to schedule pressure without ending a career.','A crewed spacecraft changes the moral character of a program. Hardware failure is no longer only lost money and scientific opportunity; people are inside the machine. Prestige therefore becomes inseparable from the institutional courage to delay.'],material:'crew escape systems, life support, human-rating standards, simulators, medical teams, recovery forces, launch vehicles, mission control and the long chain of suppliers who must all deliver exactly what they promised',people:'astronauts whose public heroism rests on private repetition, engineers signing off procedures, doctors measuring tiny risks, technicians who know one faulty seal can erase years of work and families asked to trust an institution they cannot personally inspect',argument:'Human spaceflight is justified not only by spectacle but by the systems it forces India to master: safety culture, advanced manufacturing, training and long-duration operations.',risk:'A prestige deadline can quietly become the enemy of the safety culture the program is supposed to prove. Success can also consume resources that might produce more science through robotic missions.',outside:'Other space powers will celebrate a milestone and then watch what happens next. One flight creates history; repeated safe flights create capability.',memory:'The defining image may be a crew waving after recovery. The deeper legacy will be whether the engineers who made that moment possible left behind institutions stronger than the mission patch.'});
D(/robots-first|lunar-science|planetary-lab|earth-observation|deep-space/i,{name:'robotic and scientific space',scene:['The spacecraft has no heartbeat, which means mission designers can be bolder. A probe can wait through a cold lunar night, stare at a monsoon system, cross interplanetary distance or fail in a way that becomes data rather than national mourning.','Scientific missions make quieter headlines than astronauts, but they build a different kind of prestige: papers, instruments, navigation, remote sensing and the confidence to send machines where no Indian hardware has operated before.'],material:'detectors, spectrometers, autonomous navigation, deep-space communications, Earth-observation archives, university payloads, calibration, mission software and scientists competing for scarce instrument time',people:'planetary scientists, remote-sensing researchers, instrument builders, graduate students, farmers and disaster officials who may benefit from orbital data without ever watching a launch',argument:'The robotic doctrine chooses cadence and knowledge over the singular theatre of human flight. It asks what India can learn, map and measure repeatedly.',risk:'Scientific programs can be starved by prestige politics precisely because their success is distributed across years of research rather than one televised moment.',outside:'International science rewards reliable data and instruments. Collaboration can extend reach, but dependence on foreign components or tracking networks can limit autonomy at the moments that matter most.',memory:'A probe becomes a footnote eventually. The datasets, laboratories and generation of scientists it creates can remain useful long after the spacecraft itself is silent.'});
D(/commercial-orbit|launch-market|private-space|orbital-services/i,{name:'commercial space',scene:['A commercial launch customer is less sentimental than a nation watching its flag rise. The customer asks about price, insurance, integration windows, launch cadence and what happens if a component arrives late. That bluntness is useful: it forces the space program to become an industry.','In a private workshop outside the old government centres, engineers build components that would once have required a public-sector procurement chain. The challenge is no longer proving that a startup can make a prototype; it is proving that the hundredth unit is as trustworthy as the first.'],material:'launch manifests, insurance, payload integration, licensing, private capital, standardized interfaces, supplier quality, satellite operations and the customer service nobody puts on a mission poster',people:'startup founders, technicians leaving established firms, young engineers, investors, regulators, launch-range staff and customers who care more about reliability than national prestige',argument:'Commercial orbit treats government capability as a platform on which a market can grow, not as the only legitimate source of missions.',risk:'A rush of capital can produce fragile companies, duplicated projects and pressure to weaken safety or oversight. Too much regulation can preserve reliability by ensuring there is no industry left to regulate.',outside:'Global launch and satellite markets are brutally competitive. India wins only if customers return voluntarily after the first subsidized or politically attractive mission.',memory:'The commercial revolution succeeds when a space company can fail without the nation feeling that the space program itself has failed.'});
D(/rail|corridor|port|factory|industry|steel|semiconductor|supplier|export|compute|digital|market|finance|economic|productiv/i,{name:'economic transformation',scene:['The policy becomes physical before it becomes statistical: wet concrete at a logistics yard, a new machine arriving under tarpaulin, a server hall drawing power through the night, a bank officer deciding whether a small firm is finally worth the risk.','Economic strategy sounds clean in cabinet language. At firm level it is a thousand decisions about whether demand will last long enough to hire, borrow, train and expand.'],material:'capital, power, land, working capital, standards, freight, skills, procurement and the confidence that rules will remain intelligible long enough for investment to pay back',people:'workers, owners, engineers, lenders, migrants, suppliers and households who collectively turn a macroeconomic plan into millions of private choices',argument:'The state is trying to create capability, not merely activity. The difference appears in whether firms learn to do harder things rather than only do more of what they already know.',risk:'Growth can hide weak productivity for a surprisingly long time. Subsidy, cheap credit and protected markets can make motion look like progress.',outside:'Foreign partners will respond to scale, but they will stay for reliability. Supply chains have long memories for missed deadlines and sudden rule changes.',memory:'The economic future will eventually be compressed into charts. The people living through it will remember new commutes, new machines, wages, layoffs, debts and the first time their town appeared on somebody else’s investment map.'});
D(/./,{name:'statecraft',scene:['Some decisions announce themselves with fireworks; others enter history through a government order nobody reads outside the ministry. This one begins in the space between those extremes, where political intent has to survive contact with administration, geography and people who have their own plans.','Across India, the same policy can look like opportunity in one state, interference in another, overdue reform in a third and something too distant to matter in a fourth. National scale never eliminates local interpretation.'],material:'budgets, staff, law, procurement, state cooperation, public expectations and the patient chain of small decisions between announcement and outcome',people:'officials asked to implement the decision, political supporters who expect visible proof, skeptics looking for hidden costs and citizens who will judge the policy through the few places where it touches their lives directly',argument:'The policy matters because it changes what the state believes it is allowed and expected to do. That institutional permission can last much longer than the original program.',risk:'The most dangerous failure is not always open opposition. It is ritual compliance: everyone reports progress while adapting the policy into harmlessness.',outside:'Other governments notice the headline first and the implementation later. Their behaviour changes only when they believe the new Indian capability will persist.',memory:'If the decision endures, later generations may treat its institutions as natural. That is how contingency turns into history.'});
function context(f){const text=(f.id+' '+f.title+' '+f.summary+' '+(f.tags||'')).toLowerCase();return domain.find(d=>d.re.test(text))||domain[domain.length-1]}
const openers=[
(f,d,b)=>`${pick(d.scene,f.id,1)} ${f.title} is not presented as an item to be checked off. It is a decision about what kind of ${d.name} India is willing to build and what habits of power will be normalized while building it.`,
(f,d,b)=>`${pick(d.scene,f.id,2)} Against that background, “${f.title}” becomes less a slogan than a commitment. ${f.summary} The sentence is simple; the country required to make it true is not.`,
(f,d,b)=>`The title sounds declarative—${f.title}—but the reality underneath it is crowded with bargaining, memory and unintended consequences. ${pick(d.scene,f.id,3)} The decision therefore begins with atmosphere and argument before it ever becomes a modifier.`
];
function parentText(f){const ps=(f.parents||[]).map(id=>window.PA104_GET?PA104_GET(id):null).filter(Boolean);if(!ps.length)return `Nothing formally precedes this choice because it is part of the opening definition of the country. That does not make it ahistorical: it inherits every unresolved bargain, success and resentment already present in India in 2026.`;if(ps.length===1)return `This decision grows directly out of “${ps[0].title}.” The earlier choice changes what now seems politically imaginable. Ideas that would once have looked extreme, premature or technically impossible acquire constituencies, budgets and language of their own.`;return `The path reaches this point only after ${ps.map(x=>'“'+x.title+'”').join(' and ')}. That convergence matters. Different institutions arrive here for different reasons, and the coalition supporting the decision can fracture later if one side discovers that the shared destination means something different in practice.`}
function childText(f){const cs=(window.PA104_FOCUSES||[]).filter(x=>(x.parents||[]).includes(f.id));if(!cs.length)return `There is no neat arrow after this in the same branch. That is appropriate. Some national choices are end states rather than stepping stones: their real successors are the budgets, habits and political myths they leave behind.`;if(cs.length===1)return `The immediate institutional inheritance passes into “${cs[0].title}.” That later choice will not start from zero; it will inherit the winners, losers, assumptions and administrative machinery created here.`;return `From here the future fractures again. ${cs.slice(0,4).map(x=>'“'+x.title+'”').join(', ')}${cs.length>4?', among other choices':''} become possible, which means this decision is valuable partly because it changes the menu of futures rather than merely improving a number.`}
function blurb(f){const d=context(f),b=branch[f.branch]||branch.root;const forms=[
()=>`${cap(d.name)} becomes lived politics: ${f.summary} The bargain is not only over the objective, but over who gets to define success once the announcement leaves Delhi.`,
()=>`${f.summary} Behind the slogan sit ${d.material}; the route succeeds only if those ordinary mechanisms can carry the political weight placed on them.`,
()=>`${pick(d.scene,f.id,9).split('. ')[0]}. ${f.summary} The choice will leave institutions—and people—different even if the headline goal is achieved.`
];return pick(forms,f.id,4)()}
function lore(f){const d=context(f),b=branch[f.branch]||branch.root;const o=pick(openers,f.id,1)(f,d,b);const p2=`What the leadership is really choosing is a method of power. ${f.summary} In ${b.world}, that commitment changes the incentives surrounding ${d.material}. ${d.argument} The argument is not academic; it determines who gets invited into the room, whose objections count as information and which costs can be postponed without becoming visible.`;const p3=`${parentText(f)} The transition is felt first by ${d.people}. They do not encounter “${f.title}” as a focus icon. They encounter a revised contract, a changed timetable, a new office, a prohibited meeting, a cheaper shipment, a harder border crossing, a safer clinic or a promise whose credibility depends on the person standing behind the counter.`;const p4=`Administration gives the idea its final shape. ${b.institutions} have to turn direction into law, money, staffing and routine. The critical objects are stubbornly mundane: ${d.material}. Ministers can demand urgency, but a missing engineer, an unfilled post, a court challenge, a state-level veto or a broken procurement chain can still decide the outcome. The state’s real capacity appears in how it handles those unglamorous constraints.`;const p5=`The social coalition is never as unified as the podium suggests. ${b.human}. Around this specific decision, ${d.people} pull in different directions. Some want the policy to go further; some fear what it will take away; others care less about doctrine than whether the result is reliable. That last group is often the largest and the least visible in political storytelling.`;const p6=`Every route has a shadow, and this one has a specific one: ${d.risk} ${b.shadow} The most dangerous moment comes after the first visible success, when supporters begin treating criticism as evidence of disloyalty or bureaucrats learn that an optimistic report travels faster than an accurate one. A state can become more powerful and less self-aware at the same time.`;const p7=`Outside India, nobody is obliged to understand the policy in the language India uses for itself. ${d.outside} Partners, rivals and neighbours respond to capabilities and fears, not to the elegance of the domestic justification. A stronger India may gain respect and produce balancing at the same time; a more generous India may still arouse suspicion if the power difference is too large to ignore.`;const p8=`${childText(f)} ${d.memory} That is the historical weight of “${f.title}.” Its importance lies not in the instant the node is completed but in the routines, expectations and stories that remain after the original political coalition has moved on. Strategy becomes history when later generations inherit consequences whose origins they no longer remember clearly.`;return [o,p2,p3,p4,p5,p6,p7,p8]}
window.PA105_BLURB=blurb;window.PA105_LORE=lore;window.PA105_PROFILE=f=>branch[f.branch]||branch.root;
window.PA104_LORE=lore;window.PA104_PROFILE=f=>branch[f.branch]||branch.root;
})();